SQL Server HA / DR / Data Distribution

I have 5 ways to protect DB from failure:

1- Log Shipping
--Purpose: Mainly Disaster Recovery.
A Primary Server continuously takes transaction log backups. These log backups are copied to one or more Secondary Servers and restored there.

--Failover:
Log Shipping normally requires manual failover.
So:
Log Shipping is primarily a DR solution, not automatic HA.

2-Always On Availability Groups 
--Purpose: High Availability + Disaster Recovery.

An Availability Group contains one or more databases and multiple replicas.
              Availability Group
                     |
          ┌──────────┴──────────┐
          ↓                     ↓
   Primary Replica       Secondary Replica
          |                     |
       Read/Write          Read-only / DR

You can have multiple secondary replicas:
Primary    ── READ/WRITE
Secondary1 ── READ
Secondary2 ── READ

--Failover:
Depending on the configuration, Always On AG can provide:
Automatic failover
Manual failover
Synchronous or asynchronous data movement
Readable secondary replicas

3-Database Mirroring --deprecated

4-Failover Cluster Instance (FCI)
--Purpose: High Availability at the SQL Server instance level.
An FCI uses:

             Shared Storage
                  |
          ┌───────┴───────┐
          ↓               ↓
       Node 1           Node 2
          |               |
          └── SQL Server ─┘

**Only one node actively owns the SQL Server instance at a time.

For example:
Node 1
   ↓
SQL Server Instance
   ↓
Active

If Node 1 fails:
Node 1 ❌
   ↓
Failover
   ↓
Node 2
   ↓
SQL Server Instance
   ↓
Active

Important difference"
->FCI protects the SQL Server instance.
->Always On AG protects databases through replicas.

Also:
FCI traditionally relies on shared storage, while AG replicas have independent storage.

5-Replication
Purpose: Mainly data distribution, not traditional HA/failover.
Publisher: The source database where changes occur.
    |
    ↓
Distributor: Handles the distribution of replicated changes.
    |
    ↓
Subscriber: Receives and applies the changes.

For Transactional Replication, the Log Reader Agent reads relevant changes from 
the Publisher's transaction log and sends replication commands through the Distributor to the Subscriber.

Important:
Replication is not the same as Log Shipping.
=========================================================================================================================================================================
| Technology         | Main purpose      |
| ------------------ | ----------------- |
| Log Shipping       | DR                |
| Always On AG       | HA + DR           |
| Database Mirroring | Legacy HA/DR      |
| FCI                | Instance-level HA |
| Replication        | Data distribution |
=========================================================================================================================================================================
What HA/DR solution would you choose?
It depends on the business requirements. For high availability with automatic failover, I would consider Always On Availability Groups. 
For a simpler disaster recovery solution, I could use Log Shipping. 
Transaction Replication is mainly used for distributing data to subscribers, such as reporting systems, rather than providing database failover.

Can Always On and Log Shipping be used together?

Yes, they can be used together in certain architectures. 
For example, Availability Groups can provide high availability between replicas, while Log Shipping can provide an additional disaster recovery copy at another location. 
The exact design depends on the SQL Server version, topology, RPO, RTO, and business requirements.

